MESA 26.04.1 patch: place pulse-data double points using composition gradients

When add_double_points_to_pulse_data is enabled (EM sets it, with
threshold_grad_mu_for_double_point = 1), MESA places double points
where |dln(mu)/dln(P)| exceeds the threshold. It uses s%mu, which
includes the free electrons supplied by the EOS, so partial ionization
zones (where the composition is uniform) also trigger double points.
Each one inserts a spurious 1-4% density discontinuity into the
pulsation model, and as faces cross the threshold the GYRE frequencies
jump by up to ~0.6 uHz. This stalls the bisection in
evolve_star_seismic ("too many redos") for hot (~8500 K) models.

This patch evaluates grad_mu from the composition-only mean molecular
weight abar/(1 + zbar). The same change has been proposed for MESA
main (branch pulse-double-points-composition).

Apply from the top of the MESA 26.04.1 tree, then rebuild star:

  cd $MESA_DIR
  patch -p1 < /path/to/em/patches/mesa-26.04.1-double-points.patch
  cd star && ./mk && ./export

then relink EM (make clean && make).
--- a/star/defaults/controls.defaults
+++ b/star/defaults/controls.defaults
@@ -626,7 +626,10 @@
       ! threshold_grad_mu_for_double_point
       ! ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 
-      ! threshold in grad_mu = dln(mu)/dln(P) for a double point to be written
+      ! threshold in grad_mu = dln(mu)/dln(P) for a double point to be written. Here
+      ! mu is the mean molecular weight of the composition alone (``abar/(1 + zbar)``,
+      ! i.e. assuming full ionization), so that ionization zones do not trigger double
+      ! points
 
       ! ::
 
--- a/star/private/pulse_utils.f90
+++ b/star/private/pulse_utils.f90
@@ -48,6 +48,7 @@
 
     logical, parameter :: dbg = .false.
 
+    real(dp) :: mu_comp(s%nz)
     real(dp) :: grad_mu(s%nz)
     logical  :: mask(s%nz)
     integer  :: k
@@ -61,16 +62,23 @@
 
     if (s%add_double_points_to_pulse_data) then
 
-       ! Calculate grad_mu
+       ! Calculate grad_mu from the mean molecular weight of the
+       ! composition alone, assuming full ionization. Using s%mu
+       ! (which includes the free electrons supplied by the EOS)
+       ! would also flag ionization zones, where the composition is
+       ! uniform; double points placed there introduce spurious
+       ! density discontinuities
 
+       mu_comp = s%abar(1:s%nz)/(1d0 + s%zbar(1:s%nz))
+
        grad_mu(1) = 0d0
 
        do k = 2, s%nz-1
-          grad_mu(k) = log(s%mu(k)/s%mu(k-1))/log(s%Peos(k)/s%Peos(k-1))
+          grad_mu(k) = log(mu_comp(k)/mu_comp(k-1))/log(s%Peos(k)/s%Peos(k-1))
        end do
 
        if (include_last_face) then
-          grad_mu(k) = log(s%mu(s%nz)/s%mu(s%nz-1))/log(s%Peos(s%nz)/s%Peos(s%nz-1))
+          grad_mu(k) = log(mu_comp(s%nz)/mu_comp(s%nz-1))/log(s%Peos(s%nz)/s%Peos(s%nz-1))
        else
           grad_mu(k) = 0d0
        end if
